Here's the core list, explained plainly — these are the properties that actually define "cloud native," not just "runs on a cloud provider."

## 1. Containerization
Your app packaged into a standardized, portable unit (Docker/OCI image) that runs the same way everywhere — your laptop, staging, production. This is the foundation everything else builds on.
*You have this.*

## 2. Orchestration
Something automatically manages where containers run, restarts them on failure, and reschedules them if a node dies — Kubernetes is the standard here. You don't manually decide "run this container on this server."
*You have this (K8s cluster).*

## 3. Microservices / loose coupling
The system is split into independently deployable services that talk over network APIs (REST, gRPC, queues) rather than one giant monolith. Each piece can be built, scaled, and deployed on its own schedule.
*You have this (gateway / queue / workers are separate).*

## 4. Elastic, automated scaling
The system adds/removes capacity automatically based on real demand signals (CPU, memory, queue depth, request rate) — not manually by a human watching a dashboard.
*You have this (HPA on queue depth).*

## 5. Self-healing / resilience
The system detects failures (crashed pod, hung process, failed health check) and recovers automatically — restarts, reschedules, reroutes traffic — without a human intervening.
*I have this*

## 6. Statelessness
Compute instances (pods) hold no critical state locally. If a pod dies, nothing is lost, because state lives in external stores — databases, queues, object storage. This is *what makes* self-healing safe.
*Partially — depends on whether your queue does proper ack/redelivery, worth verifying.*

## 7. Declarative infrastructure ("infrastructure as code")
Your infrastructure is described in config files (YAML, Terraform) describing the *desired end state*, and a tool reconciles reality to match it — rather than you SSH-ing in and running commands by hand.
*Depends whether your K8s manifests are applied via IaC/GitOps or manually with `kubectl apply`.*

## 8. Automated CI/CD
Code changes flow from commit → build → test → deploy automatically, with immutable artifacts (you rebuild a new image, you don't patch a running container).
*Unknown from what you've described — worth checking if you have this.*

## 9. Observability
You can see what's happening across the whole distributed system — metrics (aggregate health), logs (what happened), and ideally traces (following one request across services) — not just "SSH in and read a log file."
*You have metrics (Grafana). Logs/traces likely still a gap.*

---

**The honest summary for your video:** you have strong coverage of orchestration, elastic scaling, and observability (metrics). Your two biggest legitimate gaps are self-healing (fixable with probes, ~30 min) and verified statelessness (fixable by testing/demoing the pod-kill scenario). CI/CD and full declarative infra are likely partial or missing — naming that honestly is fine and shows you understand the full picture rather than just the part you built.